---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const pageTitle = 'About Typeframe — An Independent Typographic Studio';
const pageDescription =
  'Typeframe is a browser-based typographic studio for setting quotes, code, notes, and posts as images. How it works, what it deliberately does not do, and who makes it.';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  url="https://typeframe.pages.dev/about/"
>
  <main class="page">
    <div class="prose-wrap">
      <header class="prose-header">
        <p class="page-kicker">About</p>
        <h1 class="prose-title">Words deserve better than a screenshot.</h1>
        <p class="page-lead">
          Typeframe is a small studio for setting text as an image. It exists
          because a sentence worth reading deserves more than a cropped
          screenshot with a cursor still blinking in the corner.
        </p>
        <p class="prose-meta">Independent tool · Built by Divyanshu · November 2026</p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <section>
          <h2>Why it exists</h2>
          <p>
            Most visual tools sit at one of two extremes. Full design
            applications can do anything, provided you already know how to use
            them. Screenshot utilities are immediate, but the result usually
            looks like a screenshot: browser chrome, a selection highlight, and
            whatever line height the original page happened to use.
          </p>
          <p>
            Typeframe is the space between. One canvas, a short list of type and
            surface controls, and an export button. No dashboard to configure, no
            template marketplace to browse, and no account standing between you
            and the file.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>How it works</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Rendering happens on your device.</strong> The preview is
              drawn with the browser's Canvas API. Raster exports run through a
              local worker, so even a 4× render never leaves the machine.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Drafts are local.</strong> The current composition is saved
              to local storage and encoded into the URL, which is why a shared
              link can reopen the same design. Clearing site data removes the
              draft.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Type is self-hosted.</strong> Every typeface ships with the
              site as a WOFF2 file. There are no Google Fonts requests, and no
              third-party scripts at render time.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>SVG is a first-class export.</strong> Vector output keeps
              type as text and surfaces as shapes, while PNG, JPEG, and WebP are
              available at 1×, 2×, and 4×.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Because the work happens in the browser, there is no render queue,
            no upload progress bar, and no server holding a copy of your draft.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>The type</h2>
          <p>
            Seven typefaces cover the range from display serif to working
            monospace. Each one is included for a reason, not to pad a dropdown.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Instrument Serif</strong> — high contrast, editorial, and best at large sizes.</li>
            <li><strong>Source Serif 4</strong> — a readable text serif for longer notes.</li>
            <li><strong>Playfair Display</strong> — a magazine display face with strict vertical stress.</li>
            <li><strong>Cormorant Garamond</strong> — light, classical, and generous on the page.</li>
            <li><strong>Inter</strong> — a neutral interface sans that stays legible at small sizes.</li>
            <li><strong>IBM Plex Sans</strong> — a technical sans with a little more character than Inter.</li>
            <li><strong>JetBrains Mono</strong> — a monospace designed for code, with clear punctuation.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Variable weights are used where the family provides them, so the
            weight slider moves smoothly instead of snapping between a few
            static cuts.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>What it doesn’t do</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>No accounts, cloud sync, or shared workspace.</li>
            <li>No image hosting: exports are files on your device.</li>
            <li>No repository connection or code execution for code cards.</li>
            <li>No multi-page PDF carousels or slide decks.</li>
            <li>No tables, footnotes, or LaTeX in Markdown mode.</li>
            <li>No advertising, analytics, or tracking scripts.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Those omissions are deliberate. Every feature that would require an
            account, a database, or a third-party script has to earn its place
            against the tool's two main promises: it is fast, and it is private.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Who makes it</h2>
          <p>
            Typeframe is designed and built by <strong>Divyanshu</strong>, an
            independent web engineer. It is maintained as a small, focused tool
            rather than a venture-backed product, which is why there is no pricing
            page and no growth loop.
          </p>
          <p>
            Corrections, bug reports, and typeface suggestions are welcome at
            <a href="mailto:support@typeframe.pages.dev">support@typeframe.pages.dev</a>
            or <a href="https://x.com/dvynshuu" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">@dvynshuu</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <div class="page-actions prose-actions">
        <a class="btn-solid" href="/">Open the studio</a>
        <a class="btn-ghost" href="/contact/">Contact</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
